diff --git a/OPENVTS-README.md b/OPENVTS-README.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/OPENVTS-README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+# Open VTS Terminal — your own LLM API
+
+A modified Warp client for Windows x64, configured for the Open VTS Qwen API. Download the completed Windows artifact from the fork’s **Build Open VTS Windows installer** workflow. `OpenVTSTerminalSetup.exe` is the installer; a source archive by itself does not contain a compiled application.
+
+## API configuration
+
+The new `openvts` binary sends its built-in Agent Mode requests directly to:
+
+- Endpoint: `http://llm.openvts.io/v1/chat/completions`
+- Model: `Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GPTQ-Int4`
+- System prompt: `You are a Software Developer.`
+- `chat_template_kwargs.enable_thinking`: `true`
+- Temperature: `0.1`
+- `max_tokens`: `131000`
+- `stream`: `false`
+
+Edit `openvts-llm.json` beside the built executable to change configuration. `OPENVTS_LLM_CONFIG` can specify another config file. Tokens are never stored in this JSON. The supplied HTTP URL sends prompts and credentials without TLS; use an HTTPS endpoint when your server provides one.
+
+## Build on Windows x64
+
+1. Install Visual Studio 2022 Build Tools with Desktop development with C++, the Windows SDK, Rust/rustup, Git, CMake, LLVM/Clang and Protocol Buffers (`protoc`). Rust 1.92.0 is selected by this repository. See the original `script/windows/bootstrap.ps1` and project build documentation for platform prerequisites.
+2. Open a Developer PowerShell for VS 2022. Ensure `cargo`, `rustup`, `git`, `cmake` and `protoc` are on PATH. Set `LIBCLANG_PATH` to your LLVM bin directory if necessary.
+3. Run `BUILD-WINDOWS.cmd` from the extracted source. Logs are written to `build-logs/stdout.log` and `build-logs/stderr.log`.
+4. The wrapper enforces a **30-minute limit on the entire worker process and its child processes**, including tests, downloads, compilation and packaging. It stops the process tree on timeout. This large codebase may require another run using the retained compilation cache. No automatic retry is performed.
+5. On success, `dist/OpenVTSTerminal-<timestamp>.zip` contains `OpenVTSTerminal.exe`, required runtime DLLs, OpenConsole, resources, configuration, launch scripts and licenses. Keep the whole directory together; the EXE alone is not the complete distribution.
+
+The script first runs the adapter tests and Clippy, then compiles the actual Warp application with `openvts,gui,release_bundle`. Dependency resolution updates the root lockfile to include the new local adapter; review and retain the resolved lockfiles after the first successful build. No dependencies were silently upgraded in the delivered original lockfile. The build is unsigned. With Inno Setup 6 installed, run `BUILD-WINDOWS.cmd -Installer` to also produce `dist/OpenVTSTerminalSetup.exe`.
+
+## Start and configure your token
+
+Extract the completed distribution, close any earlier instance, and run `Start-OpenVTS.cmd`. Enter your actual API token without `Bearer `. The launcher hides input and supplies the token to the application's process environment; it does not write the token to disk. Alternatively set `OPENVTS_LLM_API_KEY` in your launch environment and run the EXE. The placeholder from the example is intentionally rejected. Restart the application when changing credentials.
+
+## Scope of the integration
+
+- A separate application ID, log name and Windows instance mutex isolate this build from Warp OSS.
+- Built-in Agent Mode uses the configured API; errors do not retry or fall back to another model provider.
+- Local single-task conversation history, reasoning output and shell tool results are converted between native Warp messages and Chat Completions messages.
+- Responses are buffered and validated before any tool calls reach the UI. Cancellation drops the pending API request. Request and response payload limits are 8 MiB and 16 MiB.
+- One tool is supported: `run_shell_command`. It is advertised only when the client supports it and `tools_enabled` is true. Shell calls are always marked risky and not read-only; existing application approval rules still apply. The model cannot override these flags. The server must support OpenAI-compatible function calling for agent command execution; set `tools_enabled` to false for plain chat.
+- The supplied source is Warp's open-source client. Its hosted orchestration service is not in the upload. Cloud agents/Oz, Warp Drive, collaboration, hosted indexing and other hosted features are not reproduced. Hosted service roots are disabled for this binary. Some original Warp UI and branding remain.
+- This candidate supports JSON responses, normal prompts, shell-command tool-result cycles and a single root task. Streaming/SSE, specialized file tools, multiple agent tasks and all hosted message variants are not implemented. Unsupported history/input fails explicitly; start a new conversation.
+
+## Validation and limitations
+
+The build script runs the adapter tests, including a local HTTP test of the API request and native shell-tool response, and Clippy before compiling the desktop application. A successfully published workflow artifact has also passed a silent installation and settings-schema generation from the installed executable. The exact results are recorded in the GitHub Actions run; a pending or failed run has no verified installer.
+
+A real Windows GPU session, interactive shell approval/cancellation, and a request using your actual API token still require verification on your machine. No actual token was supplied for testing. This is an unsigned custom build, not a production-certified release.
+
+Original AGPL/MIT licenses and third-party notices are retained. This is a modified client, not an official Warp distribution.
+
+## Windows runner build
+
+`.github/workflows/openvts-windows.yml` prepares a Windows Server 2022 runner, runs the targeted adapter tests and Clippy, compiles the application, creates an Inno Setup installer, and checks a silent installation plus settings-schema generation from the installed binary. It uploads completed installer/portable artifacts only after those checks pass. A real GPU session and a live authenticated LLM request still need verification.
+
+The workflow has a 30-minute job limit; its build subprocess is stopped after 23 minutes so logs can still be uploaded. Compilation caches are retained when possible. No LLM token or signing certificate is required to build. The build recipe is in `fleetstack2011/openvts-terminal`. Its bootstrap workflow checks out the exact uploaded Warp revision and applies `openvts-custom-llm.patch`. The resulting source ZIP contains the actual modified files. The standalone workflow in this source ZIP can be started from the Actions tab after committing this source to a repository. It creates workflow artifacts, not a public release.
